<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vanua'aku Pati</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1971">1971</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as New Hebrides National Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vanua'aku Pati is a political party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>, founded in 1971 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-lini">Walter Lini</a> and other Anglican-educated leaders and renamed from the New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> in 1977. It campaigned for land rights and independence from the Anglo-French condominium, won the elections of 1979 and led the country to independence in 1980, with Lini as the first prime minister. It dominated government until 1991.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-lini">Walter Lini</a> founded the party on 17 August 1971 as the New Hebridean Cultural Association, and it took the name New Hebrides National Party later that year. It was one of two parties formed as the islands moved towards independence: the National Party had the support of British interests, while the rival Union of New Hebrides Communities was backed by French interests. The party won the elections of 1975, and after its renaming in 1977 Lini became chief minister following the pre-independence elections of 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Lini remained prime minister until 1991, when the party split and he was among those who left it. It lost its hold on government but stayed strong, and in the following decade two of its members, Donald Kalpokas and Edward Natapei, served as prime minister. Its seats then fell from 18 in 1998 to 14 in 2002 and 8 in 2004, a setback that forced Natapei to resign. After a further split it reunited in December 2004 and backed the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-lini">Walter Lini</a>'s brother Ham Lini.</p><p>In September 2008 it won 11 of the 52 seats, becoming the largest party in Parliament, and Natapei returned as prime minister until a vote of no confidence removed him on 2 December 2010. Joe Natuman of the party became prime minister in 2014 for about a year. After the 2020 election its new leader, Bob Loughman, was appointed prime minister and held the office until August 2022, when he asked the President to dissolve Parliament to avoid a vote of no confidence; after the snap election the party went into opposition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The name means Party of My Land or Party of Our Land, from the word vanua. The party supports socialist economic policies and draws most of its support from English speakers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vanua'aku Pati” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Vanuaʻaku Pati</b> under Walter Lini led the drive for independence, opposed by the Nagriamel movement of Jimmy Stevens, which attempted secession on Espiritu Santo in 1980.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-hebrides-condominium">New Hebrides Condominium</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Vanua'aku Pati</b> won the 1979 elections, and independence came on 30 July 1980 under Prime Minister Walter Lini, with a ceremonial president replacing the Resident Commissioners.</blockquote><a href="/state/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a></li></ul></section>
